    "payload": {
      "content": "When evaluating Significance (SIGN), prioritize the breadth of the problem addressed (SIGN-BRD) and the magnitude of improvement over state-of-the-art (SIGN-SOT). A key pitfall is accepting empirical analyses on simple, low-dimensional benchmarks (e.g., MNIST) as sufficient evidence for domain impact (SIGN-DOM); reviewers should demand validation on complex, real-world datasets to ensure generalizability. Furthermore, distinguish between diagnostic observations and actual methodological contributions; if a paper identifies a trade-off or vulnerability without proposing a mitigation or new algorithm, its SOT and practical impact (SIGN-IMP) are significantly limited. Always assess whether the work offers actionable solutions for real-world deployment scenarios, particularly in safety-critical domains, rather than just theoretical insights.",
